From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 11:27:10 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] uml: commit Darwin guest RAM on demand

The Darwin runtime backs guest RAM with anonymous memory, and nothing
gives it back, so a guest that once peaked keeps that memory for the rest
of the boot. On physical iOS the whole of mem= is wired before the guest
starts, so the footprint never moves at all.

Make the guest tell the host which RAM it uses. Guest RAM starts
uncommitted; mem_init() commits what memblock handed out before the page
allocator existed, and arch_alloc_page() commits a chunk the first time
the allocator hands out one of its pages, outside the zone lock and
before the caller can touch it. A host that wires guest RAM wires it
then. Free-page reporting releases free chunks: the host drops them from
the process footprint and unwires them on iOS. The host keeps a released
range's contents only until it wants the memory and does not count it,
so it has to be committed again before reuse; skipping that would let
the emulator exceed its memory limit unseen and leave reused guest pages
unwired. One bit per 32-page chunk records what the host holds, and the
hook costs the allocation fast path one load once every chunk is
committed.

Reporting hands each free list over a sixteenth at a time, two seconds
apart, so a large release reaches the host over half a minute.
uml_physmem_release_free() runs it at once, pass after pass, for a
caller about to let the host stop scheduling the guest.

This adds darwin_uml_physmem_release() and darwin_uml_physmem_commit()
to the bridge, version 12.
